;; screen-grab manifest for the ONE demo that can be recorded as a GIF.
;; https://github.com/burinc/b12n-screen-grab
;;
;; Why only one, and it is NOT the reason first recorded here. Synthetic input
;; does reach an AppKit window: cgevent's :tap-by-role presses a control through
;; the accessibility API (AXPress), and once a field is focused that way a
;; synthetic :type lands in it. Both measured — a flow drove the counter 0 -> 3,
;; and typing 100 into the converter produced 212. A 21-frame GIF of the counter
;; was recorded that way.
;;
;; The actual blocker is the accessibility TREE, not input. From a cold,
;; unattended launch the app exposes only a recursive AXApplication with no
;; AXWindow child, so a flow finds nothing to press. It populated only after a
;; human had interacted with the window. Activating via System Events, clicking
;; the title bar synthetically, and polling for 30s all failed to wake it.
;; Until that is solved, every demo needing interaction stays a still.
;;
;; `timer` is the exception, and glitter's own manifest agrees: its timer entry
;; is `{:duration 10}` with NO :input either. The demo animates on its own — a
;; repeating NSTimer drives the progress bar — so a recording of it is a real
;; recording of the thing working, with nothing synthesised.
{:driver  :spawn
 :capture :cgevent-gif
 :out-dir "docs/demos"
 ;; Its OWN ledger and README, not the stills manifest's. screen-grab keys a
 ;; ledger entry by :id alone — the format is not part of the key — so a record
 ;; run and a shot run sharing an out-dir overwrite each other's entries, and
 ;; the second one to finish rewrites README.md with only its own items. Hit
 ;; live: one record run reduced a six-still README to a single GIF line.
 :ledger  "docs/demos/gifs-ledger.edn"
 :readme  "docs/demos/GIFS.md"
 :defaults {:duration 10 :fps 10 :width 520 :warmup 12}

 :examples
 [{:id    "timer"
   :group "7guis"
   :run   "jolt -M:timer"
   :src   "examples/glitter_uikit/timer.clj"
   :description "The 7GUIs Timer, recorded live — the bar advances on its own, no synthetic input involved."}]}
